Input: {"question": "When were the Gibraltar Barracks established?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d00", "start": 0, "end": 101, "text": "Gibraltar Barracks, Bury St Edmunds Gibraltar Barracks is a military installation in Bury St Edmunds."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d00", "start": 102, "end": 135, "text": "The barracks were opened in 1878."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d00", "start": 136, "end": 255, "text": "Their creation took place as part of the Cardwell Reforms which encouraged the localisation of British military forces."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d00", "start": 256, "end": 353, "text": "The barracks became the depot for the two battalions of the 12th (East Suffolk) Regiment of Foot."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d00", "start": 354, "end": 477, "text": "Following the Childers Reforms, the regiment evolved to become the Suffolk Regiment with its depot in the barracks in 1881."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d00", "start": 478, "end": 612, "text": "The barracks went on to become the regional centre for infantry training as the East Anglian Brigade Depot in 1960 and remain the home"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d01", "start": 0, "end": 132, "text": "of the former Suffolk Localisation depot , ( now the Suffolk Regimental Museum , qv ) , with entrance gates and low corner turrets ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d01", "start": 133, "end": 159, "text": "1878 , by the War Office ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d01", "start": 160, "end": 220, "text": "Red brick , laid in English Bond , with some stone details ."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d01", "start": 221, "end": 302, "text": "The walls are high with a high plinth and a projecting top with rounded copings ."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d01", "start": 303, "end": 367, "text": "It is divided into narrow bays by sturdy full height pilasters ."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d01", "start": 368, "end": 435, "text": "Along the south side 2 sections are rebuilt without any detailing ."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d01", "start": 436, "end": 501, "text": "Where the ground level changes the wall is stepped at intervals ."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d01", "start": 502, "end": 513, "text": "At the east"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d02", "start": 0, "end": 419, "text": "Defence Operator British Army Location Gibraltar Barracks Location within Suffolk Coordinates 52°14′53″N 00°41′54″E ﻿ / ﻿ 52.24806°N 0.69833°E ﻿ / 52.24806 ; 0.69833 Site history Built 1878 Built for War Office Architect Major HC Seddon RE [ 1 ] In use 1878-2010s Garrison information Occupants Royal Anglian Regiment Gibraltar Barracks is a former British Army installation located on Out Risbygate , Bury St Edmunds ."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d02", "start": 420, "end": 467, "text": "It is now home to the Suffolk Regiment Museum ."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d02", "start": 468, "end": 519, "text": "History [ edit ] The barracks were opened in 1878 ."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d02", "start": 520, "end": 589, "text": "[ 2 ] Their creation took place as part of the Cardwell Reforms which"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d03", "start": 0, "end": 127, "text": "moulded head, and carries a stone block, on the left with a shield dated 1920, and on the right with the interwoven letters VR."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d03", "start": 128, "end": 218, "text": "Also painted over the left gate are the almost obliterated words 'DEPOT SUFFOLK REGIMENT'."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d03", "start": 219, "end": 327, "text": "Included as part of a group with the former keep (qv), as forming the entrance to the depot, now demolished."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d03", "start": 328, "end": 435, "text": "Listing NGR: TL8427664520 Legacy The contents of this record have been generated from a legacy data system."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d03", "start": 436, "end": 504, "text": "Legacy System number: 467080 Legacy System: LBS Legal This building."}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d04", "start": 0, "end": 120, "text": "Gibraltar Barracks, Northampton Gibraltar Barracks is a former military installation at Northampton in Northamptonshire."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d04", "start": 121, "end": 264, "text": "Permanent barracks were first established in Northampton as part of the British response to the threat of the French Revolution in around 1797."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d04", "start": 265, "end": 329, "text": "The site comprised three barrack blocks and large parade ground."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d04", "start": 330, "end": 555, "text": "In 1873 a system of recruiting areas based on counties was instituted under the Cardwell Reforms and the barracks became the depot for the 48th (Northamptonshire) Regiment of Foot and the 58th (Rutlandshire) Regiment of Foot."}, "D5S5": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d04", "start": 556, "end": 661, "text": "The barracks were renamed Gibraltar Barracks at that time in recognition of the participation of the 58th"}, "D6S1": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d05", "start": 0, "end": 435, "text": "which formed part of the barracks complex Owner Ministry of Defence Operator British Army Location Gibraltar Barracks Location within Northamptonshire Coordinates 52°14′46″N 0°53′53″W ﻿ / ﻿ 52.246°N 0.898°W ﻿ / 52.246 ; -0.898 Site history Built 1797 Built for War Office In use 1797–1969 Garrison information Occupants Northamptonshire Regiment Gibraltar Barracks is a former military installation at Northampton in Northamptonshire ."}, "D6S2": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d05", "start": 436, "end": 597, "text": "History [ edit ] Permanent barracks were first established in Northampton as part of the British response to the threat of the French Revolution in around 1797 ."}, "D6S3": {"document_number": 6, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d05", "start": 598, "end": 653, "text": "[ 1 ] The site comprised three barrack blocks and large"}, "D7S1": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d06", "start": 0, "end": 143, "text": "RSME-PPP project, the Holdfast consortium redeveloped the barracks and built a new officers' mess on the site so allowing the manor to be sold."}, "D7S2": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d06", "start": 144, "end": 201, "text": "8th Engineer Brigade moved to Gibraltar Barracks in 2014."}, "D7S3": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d06", "start": 202, "end": 298, "text": "Gibraltar Barracks, Minley Gibraltar Barracks is a military installation at Minley in Hampshire."}, "D7S4": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d06", "start": 299, "end": 569, "text": "The barracks were established in the early 1970s on the opposite side of the A327 Minley Road to Minley Manor and initially accommodated an engineer brigade which relocated from Aldershot and various engineer recruit training units which transferred from Southwood Camp."}, "D7S5": {"document_number": 7, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d06", "start": 570, "end": 645, "text": "The Royal Engineers also occupied the manor itself and used it initially as"}, "D8S1": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d07", "start": 0, "end": 161, "text": "Top posts of July 16 , 2023 Reddit reReddit : Top posts of July 2023 Reddit reReddit : Top posts of 2023 Reddit Rules Privacy Policy User Agreement Reddit , Inc."}, "D8S2": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d07", "start": 162, "end": 170, "text": "© 2025 ."}, "D8S3": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d07", "start": 171, "end": 192, "text": "All rights reserved ."}, "D8S4": {"document_number": 8, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d07", "start": 193, "end": 599, "text": "Expand Navigation Collapse Navigation & nbsp ; & nbsp ; TOPICS Internet Culture ( Viral ) Amazing Animals & Pets Cringe & Facepalm Funny Interesting Memes Oddly Satisfying Reddit Meta Wholesome & Heartwarming Games Action Games Adventure Games Esports Gaming Consoles & Gear Gaming News & Discussion Mobile Games Other Games Role-Playing Games Simulation Games Sports & Racing Games Strategy Games Tabletop"}, "D9S1": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d08", "start": 0, "end": 194, "text": "'s bus and coach station , as does the cross-country service between Clacton-on-Sea and Liverpool which travels via Cambridge , Peterborough , Leicester , Nottingham , Sheffield and Manchester ."}, "D9S2": {"document_number": 9, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0453-f7de9acfbd96:d08", "start": 195, "end": 561, "text": "Twin towns [ edit ] Bury St Edmunds is twinned with : Compiègne , Oise , Picardy , France Huy , Wallonia , Liège , Belgium Kevelaer , North Rhine-Westphalia , Germany Xi'an , Shaanxi , China Affiliations [ edit ] HMS Vengeance : Royal Navy 